---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const title = "How to Evaluate a Business";
const description =
  "A framework for judging a company beyond its ratios: what it sells, whether " +
  "its advantage lasts, how it earns returns, and where the analysis usually fails.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Fundamentals / Evaluating a business">
  <h1>How to evaluate a business</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Ratios describe what a business <em>has done</em>. Evaluating it means forming
    a view on whether that continues — and that requires understanding what the
    company actually sells, to whom, and why they keep buying.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with the sentence test</h2>
  <p>
    Before any spreadsheet: can you explain, in one plain sentence, how the
    company makes money? Not the sector — the mechanism. Who pays, for what, how
    often, and what happens if they stop.
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    If you cannot write that sentence, no amount of ratio analysis will rescue
    the decision. Most poor outcomes trace back to owning something the buyer
    never really understood.
  </p>

  <h2>The five questions</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>1. what_is_sold</code><span class="meta">the revenue engine</span></div>
      <p>Product or service, one-off or recurring, discretionary or essential.
        Recurring revenue from something customers cannot easily stop buying is
        structurally more valuable than a lumpy order book, and the two deserve
        different multiples.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>2. who_pays</code><span class="meta">customer concentration</span></div>
      <p>Thousands of small customers, or three large ones? Concentration is in
        the annual report. A supplier deriving 40% of revenue from one buyer does
        not control its own pricing, whatever its margins currently say.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>3. why_here_and_not_a_competitor</code><span class="meta">the moat</span></div>
      <p>Brand, switching costs, network effects, regulatory licence, distribution
        reach, genuine cost advantage. If the honest answer is "no particular
        reason", margins will be competed away eventually.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>4. what_does_growth_cost</code><span class="meta">capital intensity</span></div>
      <p>How much must be invested to add a rupee of revenue? A business that
        grows without much new capital compounds far faster than one that must
        build a plant first. This is visible as
        <a href="/learn/roe-roce/">ROCE</a> alongside capex in the cash flow
        statement.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>5. what_would_break_it</code><span class="meta">the pre-mortem</span></div>
      <p>Write down what would have to happen for this to be a bad decision — a
        raw material spike, a regulatory change, a technology shift, the loss of
        one customer. If you cannot name anything, you have not looked hard
        enough.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Durable advantages, and the ones that only look durable</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>switching_costs</code><span class="meta">usually durable</span></div>
      <p>Software embedded in a customer's operations, a bank holding the salary
        account. Leaving is expensive or disruptive, so customers stay even when a
        cheaper option exists.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>brand_with_pricing_power</code><span class="meta">durable if real</span></div>
      <p>The test is whether prices can rise with inflation without volumes
        falling. A recognised name that still discounts to move stock does not
        have pricing power — it has advertising.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>regulatory_licence</code><span class="meta">durable until policy changes</span></div>
      <p>Limited licences create genuine protection, and also genuine dependence
        on a decision you do not control.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>scale_alone</code><span class="meta">often mistaken for a moat</span></div>
      <p>Being the largest only helps if size produces a cost advantage
        competitors cannot replicate. In commodity businesses it frequently does
        not.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>first_mover</code><span class="meta">rarely durable</span></div>
      <p>Arriving first is an advantage only if something is built with the head
        start. On its own it is a timing accident.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Reading management</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>compare_old_letters_to_outcomes</code><span class="meta">the best single test</span></div>
      <p>Read the chairman's letter from four years ago, then check what actually
        happened. Managements that state targets plainly and then discuss misses
        honestly are behaving differently from those that only ever narrate
        success.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>capital_allocation_record</code><span class="meta">what they did with the money</span></div>
      <p>Where did retained profit go — into the core business, acquisitions,
        unrelated diversification, dividends? Acquisitions outside the core are
        where a great deal of shareholder value has historically disappeared.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>related_party_transactions</code><span class="meta">in the notes</span></div>
      <p>Money moving between the listed company and promoter-owned entities
        deserves explanation. Sometimes ordinary, sometimes value leaving through
        the side door. See
        <a href="/learn/promoter-governance/">promoter holding and governance</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Where this analysis usually fails</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>extrapolating_the_recent_past</code><span class="meta">the commonest error</span></div>
      <p>Three good years get projected forward for ten. Cyclical businesses look
        best at exactly the wrong moment, which is why their
        <a href="/learn/valuation-ratios/">P/E is lowest at the peak</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>confusing_a_good_business_with_a_good_investment</code><span class="meta">price matters</span></div>
      <p>An excellent company bought at an excessive price is a poor investment.
        Quality and price are separate judgements and both have to be made.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>narrative_before_numbers</code><span class="meta">order of operations</span></div>
      <p>It is easy to fall for a story and then hunt for figures that support it.
        Read the cash flow statement before the investor presentation.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>ignoring_what_you_cannot_assess</code><span class="meta">circle of competence</span></div>
      <p>If you cannot judge whether a technology or a regulatory regime will
        hold, that is a legitimate reason to pass. Declining to have a view is a
        decision, and usually a cheap one.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ad">Advertisement</div>

  <h2>How this relates to the screener</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    It does not, and the separation is worth stating plainly. The
    <a href="/screener/">screener</a> measures price behaviour — trend, momentum,
    participation. It has no opinion on whether a business has a moat or whether
    its accounts are honest. A stock can satisfy every technical condition on this
    site while the underlying company deteriorates, and the reverse happens
    constantly. They are separate questions answered from separate documents.
  </p>

  <p class="lede">
    Next: <a href="/learn/promoter-governance/">promoter holding and governance</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/valuation-ratios/">valuation ratios</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/reading-financials/">reading a financial statement</a>
  </p>
</Base>
